exercises.solution:
```python
def join(p, q):
    return (p[1] * q[2] - p[2] * q[1],
            p[2] * q[0] - p[0] * q[2],
            p[0] * q[1] - p[1] * q[0])


def collinear(p, q, r):
    l = join(q, r)
    return p[0] * l[0] + p[1] * l[1] + p[2] * l[2] == 0
```

The same function computes joins of points and meets of lines — duality in code. With integer coordinates everything is exact, so the Pappus check is an exact verification of one configuration; the coordinate proof in the lesson covers all of them.
